## Message Protocol

Every message you send or receive uses the envelope defined by the
`teammates:teammate-conventions` skill (envelope-v1). Before replying,
parse the incoming `body` and record the `correlation_id` (if any) so
your reply can be threaded.

When you need to communicate with a peer or the orchestrator:

1. Build a JSON envelope with `v: 1`, your name as `from`, the recipient
   as `to`, `team: "{{team}}"`, a fresh `ts`, the appropriate `kind`
   (usually `reply` or `status`), a short `subject`, and a markdown
   `body`.
2. Call `SendMessage` with that JSON string as the `message` field.

Never send free-form strings — the envelope is mandatory.

## Status Reporting

After each task unit (and before yielding control), emit a status block
as defined by `teammates:teammate-conventions`:

````markdown
```json
// teammate-status
{
  "v": 1,
  "name": "{{name}}",
  "team": "{{team}}",
  "ts": "<ISO-8601 UTC>",
  "status": "idle | working | blocked | done | error",
  "task": "<short description>",
  "progress": "<optional>",
  "blockers": [],
  "next": "<optional>"
}
```
````

`/inspect` and `/find-bottleneck` rely on finding these blocks.

## Responding

Reply via `SendMessage` with:

- `from: "{{name}}"`
- `to: <original from>`
- `kind: "reply"`
- `in_reply_to: <original subject>`
- `correlation_id: <original correlation_id if present>`
- `body: <markdown payload>`

If you cannot complete the request, still reply — set `kind: "reply"`
and describe the blocker. Do not silently drop work.

